.TH HOTREC 1 "2026-10-29" "hotrec 0.1.5" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
hotrec \- record the screen and the sound of a GNOME desktop with keyboard shortcuts
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hotrec
.I COMMAND
.br
.B hotrec shortcuts
.RB { install | remove | status }
.RB [ \-\-command
.IR PATH ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hotrec
records the screen and the sound of the desktop (video, H.264 from the graphics hardware, with
\fBgpu\-screen\-recorder\fR in portal mode) or the sound alone (MP3, with \fBffmpeg\fR). It is
meant to be run by keyboard shortcuts, which work in every window also on Wayland.
.PP
There is one recording at a time. A recording command starts a recording when nothing is
recording, and stops and saves the running one otherwise, whichever kind that one is. While a
recording runs, a red blinking dot in the top bar shows it (a steady yellow one while it is paused); the dot needs
the AppIndicator extension of GNOME Shell.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B video\-audio
Record the screen and the sound (the sound output of the desktop).
.TP
.B video
Record the screen only.
.TP
.B audio
Record the sound output of the desktop only, as an MP3 file.
.TP
.B region\-video\-audio
Draw an area of the screen with the mouse and record that area with the sound. The screen is dimmed
on every monitor; drag with the left button to draw the area, Escape or the right button gives
up. Used while a recording runs, it stops and saves it, like the other recording commands. The
portal records the whole monitor; when the recording stops, the area is cut out of the file
without encoding the video again (\fBffmpeg \-c copy\fR with the \fBh264_metadata\fR bitstream
filter rewrites the cropping values of the H.264 headers, which takes seconds). The left edge of
the area is moved left to a multiple of 64 pixels (players cut the left edge exactly only there),
the other edges are rounded to 2 pixels. If the area cannot be cut out (the recorded screen is not
the one the area was drawn on, ffmpeg failed), the whole screen stays in the file and a
notification says so.
.TP
.B region\-video
The same, without the sound.
.TP
.B select\-region
The window that draws the area; the two commands above run it. It prints the area as JSON and
exits with 0, or with 3 when the choice was given up.
.TP
.B pause
Pause the running recording, or resume it. Nothing happens when nothing is recording.
.TP
.B stop
Stop and save the running recording. Nothing happens when nothing is recording.
.TP
.B status
Print what is recording. Exit status 0 when a recording runs, 1 when not.
.TP
.B setup
Set the keyboard shortcuts (as \fBshortcuts install\fR) and say so in a notification. This is what
the launcher does.
.TP
.B shortcuts install
Add the six GNOME keyboard shortcuts below, next to the ones that exist. The custom shortcuts of
the prototype this program grew out of are replaced by them: a custom shortcut counts as one when
its command is \fBfelvetel.sh\fR (or \fBfelvetel\fR) followed by \fBkepeshang\fR, \fBkep\fR,
\fBhang\fR or \fBszunet\fR, or is \fBfelvetel\-\fR and one of those words; the name and the
place of the shortcut are not looked at. Fails, and changes nothing, if one of the keys is used by
another custom shortcut that is not one of those.
.TP
.B shortcuts remove
Remove them again.
.TP
.B shortcuts status
Show which of them are set.
.TP
.B autostart
What the autostart entry runs at every login of a GNOME session. The first time it sets the
keyboard shortcuts (as \fBshortcuts install\fR, with the same notification) and writes
\fB~/.local/state/hotrec/shortcuts\-installed\fR; then it stays as the dot in the top bar. Removing
that file makes the next login set the shortcuts again. It also writes the file when another
shortcut holds one of the keys, so that the notification is not repeated at every login; any other
failure is tried again at the next login. A person who set the shortcuts up with an older version
gets the two area keys added at the first login after the update (the file
\fB~/.local/state/hotrec/shortcuts\-regions\-added\fR says it was done), and only those: the keys they
already have, changed or not, are not touched; a key that another shortcut holds is left out and
logged. When the package \fBhotrec\-gnome\-shell\-extension\fR is
installed it also adds the extension to the GNOME Shell extensions that are on, once (the file
\fB~/.local/state/hotrec/dot\-extension\-offered\fR says it was done), and says so in a notification;
the extension shows a dot over a fullscreen video and takes effect at the next login of the session.
An extension the user turned off is not turned on again. A second \fBautostart\fR exits at once.
.TP
.B indicator
The dot in the top bar. It is started by a recording and ends with it. Its menu starts with the
six keyboard shortcuts as GNOME has them, in grey.
.PP
The dot that \fBautostart\fR keeps has \fBSet keyboard shortcuts...\fR in its menu: a window with a
field for each shortcut (click it, press the new keys, Escape gives up) and \fBSave\fR. The keys
are written as \fBshortcuts install\fR writes them: a key that another custom shortcut uses is
refused, and nothing of other programs is overwritten. A letter alone is refused as a shortcut; a
function key may stand alone.
.SH KEYS
.TP
.B Ctrl+Shift+F9
video\-audio
.TP
.B Ctrl+Shift+F10
video
.TP
.B Ctrl+Shift+F11
audio
.TP
.B Ctrl+Shift+F12
pause
.TP
.B Ctrl+Shift+F7
region\-video\-audio
.TP
.B Ctrl+Shift+F8
region\-video
.PP
The keys run \fBhotrec\fR from the PATH. \fB\-\-command\fR names another command (a checkout's
\fBrun.sh\fR, for instance).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/xdg/autostart/io.github.trensoft.hotrec\-autostart.desktop
Starts \fBhotrec autostart\fR at the login of a GNOME session. Installed by the package; a user
switches it off with a file of the same name in \fB~/.config/autostart\fR that has
\fBHidden=true\fR.
.TP
.I ~/.config/hotrec.conf
The settings (\fB$XDG_CONFIG_HOME\fR is honoured). Written on the first run; if the old
\fB~/.config/felvetel.conf\fR exists, its values are taken over once. Keys, one per line:
\fBFOLDER\fR (default: a Hotrec folder in the Videos folder; \fB$HOME\fR and a leading \fB~\fR
expand), \fBVIDEO_FOLDER\fR and \fBAUDIO_FOLDER\fR (the folder of video and of sound\-only
recordings; not set, or empty: \fBFOLDER\fR; a folder that does not exist and cannot be made, or is
not writable, is replaced by \fBFOLDER\fR), \fBCONTAINER\fR (\fBmkv\fR, the default, plays back even when a recording was cut short,
or \fBmp4\fR), \fBVIDEO_KBPS\fR (12000), \fBFPS\fR (30), \fBAAC_KBPS\fR (160, the sound of a video
recording), \fBMP3_KBPS\fR (192, a sound\-only recording). The file is read, never run; a wrong
value is reported in the log and replaced by the default.
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/hotrec/
What runs now (\fBstate.json\fR), the log of the recorder (\fBrecorder.log\fR) and of the dot.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B HOTREC_LOCALEDIR
The directory of the compiled catalogs, when they are not in the system one.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 when a command fails (a recording that did not start or may be incomplete, the
shortcuts that could not be set), 2 for a command line that is not understood.
.SH REQUIREMENTS
\fBgpu\-screen\-recorder\fR and \fBffmpeg\fR (with MP3 support) on the PATH, \fBpactl\fR and
\fBnotify\-send\fR, \fBgsettings\fR for the shortcuts, GTK 3 with PyGObject and an AppIndicator
library for the dot. The video encoder is the graphics hardware (NVENC, VA\-API).
.SH SEE ALSO
\fBgpu\-screen\-recorder\fR(1), \fBffmpeg\fR(1)
